Author: Thomas Dohmke <thomas@dohmke.de>
Date:   Thu Aug 13 06:33:32 2026 -0700

    fix: close Codex's review findings on the escaping guard

    A second review found five more one-line-record sinks, a raw-C1 gap in the core,
    a VERIFY contract break introduced by the previous commit, and a bug in the
    sweep itself. Each was reproduced before being fixed, and each fix was checked
    by removing it and confirming a test goes red.

    The sweep was wrong, not just incomplete. It asserts "no ESC in stdout", but the
    diff renderer emits its OWN SGR sequences when colour is on — so under
    FORCE_COLOR the diff subtests failed on this tool's legitimate output. It passed
    locally only because NO_COLOR happened to be set in the environment. Colour is
    now pinned off for the sweep, which is what makes it test one property:
    that nothing the REPOSITORY supplied reaches the terminal as a control sequence.
    The coloured path keeps its own test, where every surviving ESC must be an SGR
    code this tool wrote.

    Stray 8-bit C1 bytes are now escaped. The C1 branch only recognised the
    two-byte UTF-8 form, but a Git pathname is a byte string, so a raw 0x9b can
    arrive on its own and a terminal in an 8-bit locale reads it as CSI. Doing this
    safely meant making the scan UTF-8 aware rather than per-byte: the middle byte
    of U+65E5 is 0x97, squarely inside the C1 range, so a naive check would have
    mangled every CJK identifier it met. escapedAt now returns the width of the
    sequence it judged, and both the scan and the rewrite step over valid runes
    instead of reading their interiors. Stray bytes above 0x9f are left alone —
    they are not controls in any encoding, and rewriting them would corrupt
    Latin-1-encoded sources for no gain.

    VERIFY no longer shows a command it cannot honour. The previous commit escaped
    the rendered command string, which kept the line honest but left a command that
    no longer named the file it was derived from — and "runnable as written" is that
    field's whole contract. A command built around a control-byte path cannot be
    both runnable and safe to display, so it is not emitted at all: the residual
    floor already exists to say "nothing derivable here", and that is the truthful
    answer for a repository whose filenames cannot be printed.

    The five remaining sinks are the same one-line-record class as before, in places
    the first two reviews missed: formatCallSiteLocation (which names the call site,
    so it does not delegate to formatNeighborEndpoint and had to be escaped
    separately), disambiguationSelectors (where a forged newline would also offer a
    --file argument that is not the file), agentDiagnosticPath and the shared
    completeness renderer (whose subject is an unparseable file, printed ahead of
    the ranking), defRelatedLine, and the impact co-change entries (bare pathnames,
    the one section with no symbol name to anchor the reader's eye).

